The code statements that lead to the bug are:
skb = napi_alloc_skb(&emac->napi_rx, xdp->data_end - xdp->data_hard_start);
skb_put(skb, pkt_len);
skb_mark_for_recycle(skb);
Reason:
The first two lines allocate a new skb and set its length but never copy the received packet data from the XDP buffer into the skb. This causes uninitialized heap memory to be passed up the stack, leaking kernel heap contents. The third line marks the skb for recycle even though the skb was allocated via napi_alloc_skb (backed by NAPI page frag, not page_pool), leading to page_pool state corruption when the skb is freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
skb_copy_to_linear_data(skb, xdp->data, pkt_len);
(removal of) skb_mark_for_recycle(skb);
Reason:
Adding skb_copy_to_linear_data copies the actual packet data from the XDP buffer into the skb's linear data area, ensuring the skb contains the received packet and no heap memory leak occurs. Removing skb_mark_for_recycle prevents the incorrect recycling of a non-page_pool skb, avoiding page_pool state corruption.

